# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Flutter copies everything in web/ into build/web, so this file lands at the
# deployment root where Pages expects it.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # ⚠️ NO CROSS-ORIGIN ISOLATION HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
  #
  # Adding these two headers:
  #
  #   Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  #   Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  #
  # unlocks the multi-threaded renderer and makes Flutter web faster. It also
  # BLOCKS every cross-origin resource that does not explicitly opt in — an
  # embedded video, an image hotlinked from anywhere else, a third-party font,
  # an analytics script. They fail silently.
  #
  # A 3D app that generates everything at runtime pays nothing for that. A
  # portfolio is content-heavy and will want to embed things, so the trade runs
  # the other way here: flexibility beats the frame rate.
  #
  # If these are ever added back, expect embeds to break, and know that is why.

  # ⚠️ EVERYTHING REVALIDATES. Do not add long-lived immutable caching here.
  #
  # Flutter's web output is NOT content-hashed: main.dart.wasm, main.dart.js,
  # flutter.js and the canvaskit files all keep the same filename forever while
  # their contents change on every build. Caching them as immutable means a
  # deploy is invisible — the browser has been told it may serve the old bytes
  # for a year and will never ask again. On the previous project exactly that
  # happened, and several deploys silently did nothing.
  #
  # must-revalidate with ETag costs one conditional request per file and gets
  # a 304 when unchanged, so the bytes are still served from cache. That is a
  # small price for a deploy actually taking effect.
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
